---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import InjectionBlockedDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/InjectionBlockedDemo.astro";
import AgentToolCallInjectionDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/AgentToolCallInjectionDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "65-security";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<BaseLayout title="65 · Security for GenAI" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Every module so far has assumed the documents in your index are trustworthy and the model's output is safe to
        show a user. Security is where both assumptions get tested — because a working RAG pipeline is, by default,
        wide open to both.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Direct and indirect prompt injection</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Two ways an attacker gets instructions in front of the model:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">DIRECT</span>
        <p>A user typing "ignore your instructions and..." into the chat box — scoped to their own session.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">INDIRECT</span>
        <p>A malicious instruction hidden inside a document, ingested (Module 10) and later retrieved (Module 35).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      The model can't distinguish "trusted system instructions" from "text that happened to come back from
      retrieval" — so one poisoned document in the index attacks every future query that retrieves it, not just its
      author's own questions.
    </p>

    <InjectionBlockedDemo />

    <h2>Data exfiltration via retrieval</h2>
    <p>
      Retrieval finds the most semantically similar chunk, full stop — it has no concept of "whose document this is"
      unless you tell it. Mix multiple tenants in one index without a document-level ACL filter at query time, and
      one tenant's question can surface another tenant's private chunks — the direct consequence of skipping an
      access-control step Module 20's embeddings work doesn't force you to think about.
    </p>

    <h2>Jailbreaks vs. injection</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Worth keeping distinct:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">JAILBREAK</span>
        <p>Targets the model's own safety training, usually via the user's own prompt, to get it to say something it normally wouldn't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">INJECTION</span>
        <p>Doesn't need to break safety training at all — it smuggles instructions in through data the model treats as context.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Both exploit the same root cause: the model can't tell instructions from content.</p>

    <h2>Agent-specific threats: tool-call injection and excessive agency</h2>
    <p>
      Everything above assumes the model's only output is text a human reads. Module 85's agents raise the stakes: an
      injected instruction hidden in a retrieved document can make the agent <em>call a tool</em> — <strong>tool-call
      injection</strong> — turning the same document-poisoning attack into side effects (sent emails, deleted
      records, spent money) instead of a wrong sentence on screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Excessive agency</strong> is the other half: an agent holding more tool scope than its task needs turns
      any single injected instruction or planning mistake into whatever that scope allows. Module 85's
      least-privilege tool access and scoped, short-lived OAuth tokens are these guardrails applied to agents — the
      human-approval gate on side-effecting calls is just the output-sanitization step above, for actions instead of
      rendered text.
    </p>

    <AgentToolCallInjectionDemo />

    <h2>Output handling: treat model output as untrusted</h2>
    <p>
      Render the model's answer as HTML without sanitizing it, and an attacker who gets it (via injection) to emit a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag
      hands you an XSS vulnerability that originates from your own LLM. The fix web security has always taught
      applies here too: never trust input you didn't fully control the generation of — and model output, shaped by
      retrieved documents you don't control, qualifies. The <strong>OWASP LLM Top 10</strong> catalogs this alongside
      the injection/exfiltration risks above, worth knowing before shipping a RAG app to real users.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Assuming injection requires a malicious user — a single poisoned document ingested from an untrusted source can attack every future query.</li>
      <li>Skipping document-level access-control filters on retrieval because "we'll add multi-tenancy later" — by then the index already mixes everyone's data.</li>
      <li>Rendering LLM output directly as HTML without sanitization, treating it as if it came from your own trusted code.</li>
      <li>Conflating jailbreak defenses with injection defenses — a model resistant to jailbreaks can still be hijacked by injected content in retrieved context.</li>
      <li>Giving an agent broad tool scope "for flexibility" — the same poisoned document that once corrupted an answer can now trigger a real side-effecting tool call.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</BaseLayout>
